= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use $\hbar=c=1$ and the <Minkowski metric> $\eta=\operatorname{diag}(1,-1,-1,-1)$ throughout. In the four-component <Dirac spinor> representation, the <gamma matrices> generate the <Clifford algebra>
$$
\boxed{\{\gamma^\mu,\gamma^\nu\}=\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu+\gamma^\nu\gamma^\mu=2\eta^{\mu\nu}I_4.}
$$
Thus $(\gamma^0)^2=I_4$, $(\gamma^i)^2=-I_4$, and matrices with distinct indices anticommute. Indices are lowered with $\eta$, so $\gamma_\mu=\eta_{\mu\nu}\gamma^\nu$ and $\not p=p_\mu\gamma^\mu$.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Take the <quantum electrodynamics> interaction density to be $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{int}}=-e\bar\psi\gamma^\mu\psi A_\mu$. The momentum-space <Feynman-gauge photon propagator>, with Lorentz indices $\mu,\nu$ and momentum $k$, is
$$
\boxed{D_{\mu\nu}(k)=\frac{-i\eta_{\mu\nu}}{k^2+i\epsilon}.}
$$
The electron-photon vertex from the <QED Feynman rules> is
$$
\boxed{-ie\gamma^\mu,}
$$
with its spinor indices connecting the incident and outgoing fermion lines. The muon-photon vertex has the same coupling. The <Feynman i-epsilon prescription> specifies the pole boundary condition; using a different overall charge-sign convention changes both vertices consistently and leaves the squared amplitude unchanged.

For the <electron-positron annihilation into a muon pair>, the exchanged <photon> has $k=p+q$ and $k^2=s$. Up to the conventional overall phase, the <scattering amplitude> is
$$
\mathcal M=\frac{e^2}{s}\,[\bar v_r(q)\gamma^\mu u_s(p)]\,[\bar u_{s'}(p')\gamma_\mu v_{r'}(q')].
$$

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

At this order there is one annihilation <tree-level Feynman diagram>. The <squared-amplitude diagram> is its product with its complex conjugate, with the same external momenta and spin labels in both factors:

\C[[
 e-(p,s) --->\          /---> mu-(p',s')
             o ~~~~~~ o
 e+(q,r) <---/          \<--- mu+(q',r')

                     times

 e-(p,s) --->\          /---> mu-(p',s')
             o ~~~~~~ o                       *
 e+(q,r) <---/          \<--- mu+(q',r')
]]

A wavy line is the virtual <photon>; the arrows show fermion-number flow. The star means complex conjugation of the entire second graph, including its external spinors. This represents $|\mathcal M|^2=\mathcal M\mathcal M^*$ for fixed spins, without adding a new physical loop to the amplitude.

For the <spin sum> in part (iv), identify each external spin label between the two copies and sum it. The <fermion spin sum> sews each spinor chain into a <gamma-matrix trace>. The two incoming unpolarized spin states are then handled by a <spin average>, giving the factor $1/4$. In particular, the diagrammatic contraction becomes
$$
\boxed{\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}=\frac{e^4}{4s^2}
\operatorname{tr}(\not q\gamma^\mu\not p\gamma^\nu)
\operatorname{tr}(\not p'\gamma_\mu\not q'\gamma_\nu).}
$$

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

First derive the needed <gamma-matrix traces> directly from the <Clifford algebra>. The <cyclic property of the trace> gives
$$
2\operatorname{tr}(\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu)
=\operatorname{tr}\{\gamma^\mu,\gamma^\nu\}
=2\eta^{\mu\nu}\operatorname{tr}I_4,
$$
so $\operatorname{tr}(\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu)=4\eta^{\mu\nu}$. For $T=\operatorname{tr}(\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu\gamma^\rho\gamma^\sigma)$, move $\gamma^\mu$ successively through the other three matrices and cycle the final term back to the front:
$$
2T=2\eta^{\mu\nu}\operatorname{tr}(\gamma^\rho\gamma^\sigma)
-2\eta^{\mu\rho}\operatorname{tr}(\gamma^\nu\gamma^\sigma)
+2\eta^{\mu\sigma}\operatorname{tr}(\gamma^\nu\gamma^\rho).
$$
Therefore
$$
\operatorname{tr}(\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu\gamma^\rho\gamma^\sigma)
=4(\eta^{\mu\nu}\eta^{\rho\sigma}-\eta^{\mu\rho}\eta^{\nu\sigma}+\eta^{\mu\sigma}\eta^{\nu\rho}).
$$
These are all the trace identities required.

For massless external <Dirac spinors>, the <fermion spin sums> are $\sum_su_s(p)\bar u_s(p)=\not p$ and $\sum_rv_r(q)\bar v_r(q)=\not q$. With the initial <spin average>, part (iii) becomes a contraction of
$$
L^{\mu\nu}=4(q^\mu p^\nu+q^\nu p^\mu-\eta^{\mu\nu}p\cdot q),\qquad
H_{\mu\nu}=4(p'_\mu q'_\nu+p'_\nu q'_\mu-\eta_{\mu\nu}p'\cdot q').
$$
Their contraction is
$$
L^{\mu\nu}H_{\mu\nu}
=32\bigl[(q\cdot p')(p\cdot q')+(q\cdot q')(p\cdot p')\bigr].
$$
The massless <Mandelstam variables> give $p\cdot p'=q\cdot q'=-t/2$ and $p\cdot q'=q\cdot p'=-u/2$, hence
$$
\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}=\frac{2e^4}{s^2}(t^2+u^2).
$$
In the <center of mass> frame each particle has energy $\sqrt s/2$, and
$$
t=-\frac s2(1-\cos\theta),\qquad u=-\frac s2(1+\cos\theta).
$$
Substitution gives the concise angular answer:
$$
\boxed{\overline{|\mathcal M|^2}=e^4(1+\cos^2\theta).}
$$

= v
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Substitute the preceding <spin average> into the supplied <differential scattering cross-section>. Since $e^4=16\pi^2\alpha^2$,
$$
\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}=\frac{\alpha^2}{4s}(1+\cos^2\theta).
$$
The angular integral is $2\pi\int_{-1}^1(1+x^2)\,dx=16\pi/3$. Consequently the <relativistic scattering cross-section> for the massless <electron-positron annihilation into a muon pair> is
$$
\boxed{\sigma=\frac{4\pi\alpha^2}{3s}.}
$$
The outgoing particles are distinct, so there is no identical-particle factor. Comparing with the requested monomial form gives
$$
\boxed{B=\frac{4\pi}{3},\qquad j=2,\quad k=-1,\quad l=0,\quad m=0.}
$$
The integrated answer depends only on the incoming invariant $s$; the angular variables $t,u$ have been integrated out.

