= Solution
{parent=i}

For a time-independent <Hamiltonian operator> and a <Schrödinger picture> observable with no explicit time dependence, the <Heisenberg picture> operator is
$$
\boxed{O_H(t)=e^{iHt}O_Se^{-iHt}.}
$$
Differentiation gives $\dot O_H=iHO_H-iO_HH=i[H,O_H]$, so
$$
\boxed{[H,O_H]=-i\frac{dO_H}{dt}.}
$$
If $O_S$ itself depends explicitly on time, one must add $e^{iHt}(\partial_tO_S)e^{-iHt}$ to $\dot O_H$; the displayed commutator relation uses the usual convention of no explicit time dependence for the observable.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a free <real scalar field>, the <Lagrangian density> contains its quadratic <kinetic term> and <mass term>:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal L_1=\frac12\partial_\mu\phi_1\partial^\mu\phi_1-\frac12m_1^2\phi_1^2
=\frac12\dot\phi_1^2-\frac12|\nabla\phi_1|^2-\frac12m_1^2\phi_1^2.}
$$
The field's conjugate momentum is $\pi_1=\partial\mathcal L_1/\partial\dot\phi_1=\dot\phi_1$. The positive sign of the time-derivative term uses the metric convention fixed in Question 1.

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Apply the <Legendre transform in mechanics> pointwise to the field <Lagrangian density>. Using $\pi_1=\dot\phi_1$, the Hamiltonian density is
$$
\mathcal H_1=\pi_1\dot\phi_1-\mathcal L_1
=\frac12\pi_1^2+\frac12|\nabla\phi_1|^2+\frac12m_1^2\phi_1^2.
$$
The free <Hamiltonian operator> is its spatial integral:
$$
\boxed{H_1=\frac12\int_{\mathbb R^3}d^3x\,[\pi_1^2+(\nabla\phi_1)^2+m_1^2\phi_1^2].}
$$
A constant vacuum-energy subtraction, if made, does not affect the commutators or the ensuing field equation.

= iv
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The <canonical quantization of a real scalar field> promotes the classical equal-time brackets to the <canonical commutation relations>, in units $\hbar=1$:
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
[\phi_1(t,\mathbf x),\phi_1(t,\mathbf y)]&=0,\\
[\pi_1(t,\mathbf x),\pi_1(t,\mathbf y)]&=0,\\
[\phi_1(t,\mathbf x),\pi_1(t,\mathbf y)]&=i\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf x-\mathbf y).
\end{aligned}}
$$
The <Dirac delta distribution> expresses locality of the conjugate variables. These are identities of operator-valued distributions, interpreted after spatial smearing where necessary.

= v
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Use the free <Hamiltonian operator> and the <canonical commutation relations>. The kinetic momentum term gives
$$
[H_1,\phi_1(t,\mathbf x)]
=\frac12\int d^3y\,[\pi_1(t,\mathbf y)^2,\phi_1(t,\mathbf x)]
=-i\pi_1(t,\mathbf x).
$$
The <Heisenberg picture> equation therefore yields $\dot\phi_1=\pi_1$.

For the momentum commutator, the mass term gives $im_1^2\phi_1(\mathbf x)$. The gradient term gives
$$
\frac12\int d^3y\,[(\nabla\phi_1(\mathbf y))^2,\pi_1(\mathbf x)]
=i\int d^3y\,\nabla\phi_1(\mathbf y)\cdot\nabla_{\mathbf y}\delta^{(3)}(\mathbf y-\mathbf x)
=-i\nabla^2\phi_1(\mathbf x),
$$
where <integration by parts> differentiates the field instead of the <Dirac delta distribution>. Thus
$$
[H_1,\pi_1]=i(m_1^2-\nabla^2)\phi_1,\qquad
\dot\pi_1=(\nabla^2-m_1^2)\phi_1.
$$
Combining the two first-order operator equations gives the <Klein-Gordon equation>:
$$
\boxed{(\partial_t^2-\nabla^2+m_1^2)\phi_1=(\Box+m_1^2)\phi_1=0.}
$$
Suitable decay or spatial smearing removes the boundary term in the integration by parts.

= vi
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

For the two free <real scalar fields>, let $\mathcal L=\mathcal L_1+\mathcal L_2$. The infinitesimal <internal rotation symmetry of two real scalar fields> has $\delta\phi_1=\theta\phi_2$ and $\delta\phi_2=-\theta\phi_1$, with constant $\theta$. Its kinetic terms are invariant because this is an orthogonal rotation of the two-component field.

Under the assumed invariance of the full <Lagrangian density>, <Noether's theorem> gives the current per unit transformation parameter:
$$
j^\mu=\sum_{a=1}^2\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu\phi_a)}\frac{\delta\phi_a}{\theta}
=\phi_2\partial^\mu\phi_1-\phi_1\partial^\mu\phi_2.
$$
The <Noether current> and <Noether charge> are therefore
$$
\boxed{j^\mu=\phi_2\partial^\mu\phi_1-\phi_1\partial^\mu\phi_2,\qquad
Q=\int d^3x\,(\phi_2\pi_1-\phi_1\pi_2).}
$$
Independent species commute at equal time, so these products require no extra ordering sign. With vanishing boundary flux, $Q$ is conserved. Part (vii) checks precisely what the symmetry assumption requires of the masses.

= vii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vii}

Differentiate the <Noether charge> and use $\dot\phi_a=\pi_a$ and the <Klein-Gordon equation> for each species. The momentum products cancel because the two species commute:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\dot Q&=\int d^3x\,[\pi_2\pi_1+\phi_2(\nabla^2-m_1^2)\phi_1
-\pi_1\pi_2-\phi_1(\nabla^2-m_2^2)\phi_2]\\
&=\int d^3x\,[\phi_2\nabla^2\phi_1-\phi_1\nabla^2\phi_2]
+(m_2^2-m_1^2)\int d^3x\,\phi_1\phi_2.
\end{aligned}
$$
The first integral is the boundary flux of $\phi_2\nabla\phi_1-\phi_1\nabla\phi_2$, so it vanishes under the same boundary condition used for charge conservation. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac{dQ}{dt}=(m_2^2-m_1^2)\int d^3x\,\phi_1\phi_2.}
$$
Conservation must hold for arbitrary configurations, rather than only a state in which this particular integral happens to vanish. Thus the <internal rotation symmetry of two real scalar fields> requires
$$
\boxed{m_1^2=m_2^2,\qquad\text{and therefore }m_1=m_2\text{ for nonnegative masses}.}
$$
Equivalently, the local divergence is $\partial_\mu j^\mu=(m_2^2-m_1^2)\phi_1\phi_2$, exhibiting exactly how unequal masses break the symmetry.

= 3
{parent=Paper 301}
{scope}

= i
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

In <phi cubed theory>, each vertex has three legs. A connected four-point <tree-level Feynman diagram> therefore needs two cubic vertices and one internal propagator. There are exactly three ways to partition the labeled external legs between these vertices:

\C[[
       s channel               t channel              u channel

 p \          / p'         p ---o--- p'            p ---o--- q'
    o--------o                  |                       |
 q /          \ q'         q ---o--- q'            q ---o--- p'

 internal: p+q             internal: p-p'          internal: p-q'
]]

Solid lines denote the <real scalar field> propagator. The labels specify which external pair meets at each vertex, so the last two graphs are different even though their unlabeled shapes agree. This is the <four-point tree amplitude in phi cubed theory>:
$$
\boxed{\text{three exchange diagrams, in the }s,t,u\text{ channels; each is of order }\lambda^2.}
$$
There is no four-point contact vertex in the given cubic interaction.

= ii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the interaction sign in this paper, the cubic <Feynman rule> is $+i\lambda$, while the scalar <Feynman propagator> is $i/(k^2-m^2+i\epsilon)$. Each exchange graph therefore contributes $-i\lambda^2/(k^2-m^2+i\epsilon)$ to $i\mathcal M$. The three graphs give
$$
\mathcal M=-\lambda^2\left(\frac1{s-m^2+i\epsilon}+\frac1{t-m^2+i\epsilon}+\frac1{u-m^2+i\epsilon}\right).
$$
The equal-mass <Mandelstam variables> satisfy $s+t+u=4m^2$, so $u-m^2=3m^2-s-t$. In the physical elastic region with $m>0$, none of these denominators vanishes, and the tree amplitude is real up to its irrelevant overall convention. Thus
$$
\boxed{|\mathcal M|^2=\lambda^4\left[\frac1{s-m^2}+\frac1{t-m^2}+\frac1{3m^2-s-t}\right]^2.}
$$
The square includes interference between all three channels; summing their individual squared contributions would omit physical cross terms. If a denominator approaches a pole, retain the <Feynman i-epsilon prescription> in the modulus before taking a limit.

= iii
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the <Lorentz-invariant phase-space measure> from the original PDF, including the $1/(2E_{p'})$ and $1/(2E_{q'})$ factors missing from the local TeX. In the <center of mass> frame put $E=\sqrt s/2$ and $k=\sqrt{s-4m^2}/2$, assuming $s>4m^2$. The spatial <Dirac delta distribution> sets $\mathbf q'=-\mathbf p'$, leaving
$$
d\Phi_2=\frac1{16\pi^2}\frac{k'^2\,dk'\,d\Omega}{E_{k'}^2}\,
\delta(\sqrt s-2E_{k'}).
$$
Since $d(2E_{k'})/dk'=2k'/E_{k'}$, the radial integral gives
$$
d\Phi_2=\frac{k}{16\pi^2\sqrt s}\,d\Omega
=\frac{\sqrt{1-4m^2/s}}{32\pi^2}\,d\Omega.
$$
The <invariant flux factor> is $2\sqrt{\kappa(s,m^2,m^2)}=2\sqrt{s(s-4m^2)}$, using the <Källén function>. Dividing the phase space by this flux yields $d\sigma/d\Omega=|\mathcal M|^2/(64\pi^2s)$, with the final-state labels retained as in the printed formula.

Let $x=\cos\theta$. Of the <Mandelstam variables>, $t$ is
$$
t=-2k^2(1-x)=-\frac{s-4m^2}{2}(1-x),\qquad
\frac{dt}{dx}=\frac{s-4m^2}{2}.
$$
The azimuthal integral contributes $2\pi$, and the angular endpoints are therefore
$$
\boxed{t_{\min}=4m^2-s,\qquad t_{\max}=0.}
$$
Changing variables gives the requested expression from the formula supplied in the paper:
$$
\boxed{\sigma=\frac1{16\pi}\int_{4m^2-s}^{0}\frac{|\mathcal M|^2}{s(s-4m^2)}\,dt,
\qquad X=\frac1{16\pi},\quad Y=4.}
$$
There is a normalization qualification: the displayed starting formula integrates over labeled final momenta and contains no $1/2!$. For the physical cross-section of two indistinguishable outgoing quanta of this <real scalar field>, the <identical-particle factor in a final-state phase-space integral> divides the full integral by two. With that convention $X=1/(32\pi)$; equivalently, integrate only one representative of each exchanged pair. The boxed value $1/(16\pi)$ follows the given formula exactly.

= iv
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

In four spacetime dimensions with $\hbar=c=1$, a <relativistic scattering cross-section> has <mass dimension> $-2$, since it is an area. The action is dimensionless, so the <Lagrangian density> has dimension four. The scalar <kinetic term> then gives $[\phi]=1$, and the cubic term gives $[\lambda]=1$.

Each term of the <four-point tree amplitude in phi cubed theory> has dimension $[\lambda^2]-[s-m^2]=2-2=0$. Hence $[|\mathcal M|^2]=0$. In the final integral,
$$
[dt]=2,\qquad [s]=[s-4m^2]=2,
$$
while the numerical factors $X,Y$ are dimensionless. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\left[\int dt\,\frac{|\mathcal M|^2}{s(s-4m^2)}\right]=2+0-4=-2=[\sigma].}
$$
Including the identical-particle factor changes only a dimensionless constant and preserves this check.

= 4
{parent=Paper 301}
{scope}

= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Under a <gauge transformation>, the <electromagnetic field tensor> changes by
$$
\delta F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu\partial_\nu\xi-\partial_\nu\partial_\mu\xi=0.
$$
The partial derivatives commute for a smooth gauge parameter, or in the distributional formulation. Thus the <Maxwell Lagrangian> depends only on a gauge-invariant tensor:
$$
\boxed{F'_{\mu\nu}=F_{\mu\nu},\qquad\mathcal L'=-\frac14F'_{\mu\nu}F'^{\mu\nu}=\mathcal L.}
$$
Strictly, the pointwise calculation requires the second derivatives to exist; smooth gauge parameters are the usual convention. Mere first differentiability in the wording can instead be understood through commuting distributional derivatives.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

A <Lorentz transformation> preserves the quadratic form of the <Minkowski metric> for every four-vector. Substituting $x'^\sigma=\Lambda^\sigma{}_{\mu}x^\mu$ gives
$$
\eta_{\sigma\tau}\Lambda^\sigma{}_{\mu}\Lambda^\tau{}_{\nu}x^\mu x^\nu
=\eta_{\mu\nu}x^\mu x^\nu.
$$
The coefficient difference is a symmetric matrix, and a symmetric quadratic form that vanishes on every vector is zero: evaluating it on basis vectors and their sums recovers each coefficient. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\Lambda^T\eta\Lambda=\eta,\qquad
\eta_{\mu\nu}=\eta_{\sigma\tau}\Lambda^\sigma{}_{\mu}\Lambda^\tau{}_{\nu}.}
$$
This is the defining metric-preservation relation of the <Lorentz group>.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Insert $\Lambda^\mu{}_{\nu}=\delta^\mu{}_{\nu}+\alpha\omega^\mu{}_{\nu}$ into the <Lorentz group> condition and keep the coefficient of $\alpha$. It gives
$$
\eta_{\sigma\nu}\omega^\sigma{}_{\mu}+\eta_{\mu\tau}\omega^\tau{}_{\nu}=0.
$$
Define $\omega_{\mu\nu}=\eta_{\mu\rho}\omega^\rho{}_{\nu}$. The displayed equation is $\omega_{\nu\mu}+\omega_{\mu\nu}=0$. Raising both indices with the symmetric <Minkowski metric> preserves this antisymmetry:
$$
\boxed{\omega_{\mu\nu}=-\omega_{\nu\mu},\qquad\omega^{\mu\nu}=-\omega^{\nu\mu}.}
$$
These are the generators of the <Lie algebra of the Lorentz group>. In particular $\omega^\mu{}_{\mu}=0$. The mixed-index matrix need not be antisymmetric in the ordinary Euclidean transpose; it is antisymmetric after lowering an index with $\eta$.

= iv
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

For the <active Lorentz transformation of a vector field>, coordinates are held fixed and $A'^\mu(x)=\Lambda^\mu{}_{\nu}A^\nu(\Lambda^{-1}x)$. Its field strength transforms as a two-index <Lorentz tensor>. The index contractions in the <Maxwell Lagrangian> are invariant by $\Lambda^T\eta\Lambda=\eta$, so the density transforms as a <Lorentz scalar>:
$$
\mathcal L'(x)=\mathcal L(\Lambda^{-1}x).
$$
For $\Lambda=I+\alpha\omega$, its inverse is $I-\alpha\omega+O(\alpha^2)$. Expanding the coordinate argument therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\delta\mathcal L(x)=-\alpha\omega^\mu{}_{\nu}x^\nu\partial_\mu\mathcal L(x).}
$$
Set $v^\mu(x)=\alpha\omega^\mu{}_{\nu}x^\nu$. Part (iii) implies $\partial_\mu v^\mu=\alpha\omega^\mu{}_{\mu}=0$. The product rule then converts the variation into a total derivative:
$$
\boxed{\delta\mathcal L=-\partial_\mu\bigl(\alpha\omega^\mu{}_{\nu}x^\nu\mathcal L\bigr).}
$$
Its integral changes the action only by a boundary term, which is the condition needed for <Noether's theorem>.

= v
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

For a translation with the sign used in the paper, the field variation at fixed coordinates is $\delta A_\rho=\beta\epsilon^\nu\partial_\nu A_\rho$, and $\delta\mathcal L=\partial_\mu(\beta\epsilon^\mu\mathcal L)$. Differentiating the <Maxwell Lagrangian> with respect to the field gradient gives
$$
\frac{\partial\mathcal L}{\partial(\partial_\mu A_\rho)}=-F^{\mu\rho}.
$$
Thus <Noether's theorem> yields $j^\mu=\epsilon_\nu T^{\mu\nu}$, where the <canonical stress-energy tensor of the Maxwell field> is
$$
\boxed{T^{\mu\nu}=-F^{\mu\rho}\partial^\nu A_\rho-\eta^{\mu\nu}\mathcal L
=-F^{\mu\rho}\partial^\nu A_\rho+\frac14\eta^{\mu\nu}F_{\rho\sigma}F^{\rho\sigma}.}
$$
It is conserved on the source-free <Maxwell equations>, but it is not generally symmetric. Indeed,
$$
T^{\mu\nu}-T^{\nu\mu}
=-F^{\mu\rho}\partial^\nu A_\rho+F^{\nu\rho}\partial^\mu A_\rho
$$
need not vanish. For example, take $A_0=a x^1$, $A_2=b x^0$, and the other components zero, with nonzero constants $a,b$. This produces constant field strength and satisfies the source-free equations, but $T^{12}=0$ and $T^{21}=ab$.

Nor is it <gauge-invariant>: while $F$ and $\mathcal L$ are unchanged, a <gauge transformation> gives
$$
\boxed{\delta_\xi T^{\mu\nu}=-F^{\mu\rho}\partial^\nu\partial_\rho\xi,}
$$
which is generally nonzero. These shortcomings motivate the improvement in part (vi); they do not contradict conservation of the <canonical stress-energy tensor>.

= vi
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

Antisymmetry gives $-F^{\rho\mu}\partial_\rho A^\nu=F^{\mu\rho}\partial_\rho A^\nu$. Combining this with the <canonical stress-energy tensor> removes the potential in favour of the <electromagnetic field tensor>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Theta^{\mu\nu}
&=-F^{\mu\rho}(\partial^\nu A_\rho-\partial_\rho A^\nu)
+\frac14\eta^{\mu\nu}F_{\rho\sigma}F^{\rho\sigma}\\
&=\boxed{-F^{\mu\rho}F^\nu{}_{\rho}+\frac14\eta^{\mu\nu}F_{\rho\sigma}F^{\rho\sigma}.}
\end{aligned}
$$
This is the <electromagnetic stress-energy tensor> in the $(+,-,-,-)$ convention. The product $F^{\mu\rho}F^\nu{}_{\rho}$ is symmetric in $\mu,\nu$, since the contracted metric is symmetric. Its expression only in $F$ makes it <gauge-invariant>. These two properties hold without using the equations of motion.

For conservation, the source-free <Maxwell equations> are $\partial_\rho F^{\rho\mu}=0$. Hence the given correction equals
$$
-F^{\rho\mu}\partial_\rho A^\nu=-\partial_\rho(F^{\rho\mu}A^\nu)
$$
on shell. This is an <antisymmetric superpotential improvement>: $B^{\rho\mu\nu}=-F^{\rho\mu}A^\nu$ is antisymmetric in $\rho,\mu$, so $\partial_\mu\partial_\rho B^{\rho\mu\nu}=0$. Conservation of the <canonical stress-energy tensor> therefore implies $\partial_\mu\Theta^{\mu\nu}=0$. Equivalently, direct differentiation gives $\partial_\mu\Theta^{\mu\nu}=-F^\nu{}_{\rho}\partial_\mu F^{\mu\rho}$ after the homogeneous <Maxwell equations> cancel the field-strength derivative terms.

There are four <conserved currents>, one for each fixed $\nu$, with charges $P^\nu=\int d^3x\,\Theta^{0\nu}$. The <Belinfante-Rosenfeld stress-energy tensor> differs from the canonical tensor only by boundary contributions to these charges when the fields decay suitably.

Finally, in four spacetime dimensions,
$$
\Theta^\mu{}_{\mu}=-F^{\mu\rho}F_{\mu\rho}+\frac44F_{\rho\sigma}F^{\rho\sigma}=0.
$$
Thus all requested properties are
$$
\boxed{\partial_\mu\Theta^{\mu\nu}=0\ \text{on shell},\qquad
\Theta^{\mu\nu}=\Theta^{\nu\mu},\qquad\delta_\xi\Theta^{\mu\nu}=0,\qquad\Theta^\mu{}_{\mu}=0.}
$$
